ass: never hash the padding of a cache key

The series hashes a key's fixed-width members as one byte run, from the
first member to the end of the last, on the premise that no key struct has
padding between two members. That holds where a pointer is as wide as a
double. On ARM EABI a double is aligned to 8 bytes in a struct of 4-byte
pointers, so every key that starts with a font pointer and a size (glyph
outlines, glyph and face metrics, shaping results) has four bytes of
padding after the pointer, and the run covered them. They hold whatever
the stack held before, so two equal keys hashed differently whenever that
differed: the lookup went to another bucket, missed, and the glyph's
outline was made again, and with a new outline its bitmap and the blurred
composite of the whole run, since those keys hold the outline's address.
The frame came out the same, late, and was reported as changed, which
makes mpv pack and upload its atlas again. Another thread's stack always
differs, so with four render threads a glyph was cached once per thread.

A run now ends where the next member does not start right after the
previous one. Where the members are contiguous the compiler folds the test
and the hash is the same value as before; on ARMv7 the padding is skipped.

On a Fire TV Stick 4K Max (4 Cortex-A55, armeabi-v7a), 3836 frames of a
typeset anime episode, 1252 of them different from the one before: 1288
reported as changed with four threads and 1262 with one, 1252 now.
scripts/test_libass_key_padding.c fills the stack with another byte before
each render of the same frame and fails on that device without this patch.

diff --git a/libass/ass_cache_template.h b/libass/ass_cache_template.h
index 39186c7..ed177fa 100644
--- a/libass/ass_cache_template.h
+++ b/libass/ass_cache_template.h
@@ -38,11 +38,14 @@
 // mixes. A STRING member (variable length, indirect storage) flushes the
 // pending run; END flushes any trailing run.
 //
-// SAFETY: the coalesced range spans exactly [first_member, last_member_end);
-// it never extends to sizeof(struct), so trailing padding is never hashed.
-// All key structs are verified to have NO internal padding between adjacent
-// fixed-width members (every adjacent pair is byte-contiguous), so no
-// uninitialized padding bytes are ever included -> equal keys still hash equal.
+// A run ends where the next member does not start right after the previous
+// one, so padding is never hashed, between members or after the last. With an
+// ABI that aligns a double or a 64-bit integer to 8 bytes in a 32-bit struct
+// (ARM EABI), a pointer followed by a double has four bytes of padding after
+// it; hashed, whatever the stack held there made equal keys hash differently,
+// and a lookup of a cached glyph missed whenever that changed (always, for
+// another thread's lookups). Where members are contiguous the compiler folds
+// the test away and one run covers them all.
 #define START(funcname, structname) \
     static ass_hashcode funcname##_hash(void *buf, ass_hashcode hval) \
     { \
@@ -54,7 +57,10 @@
             run_beg = run_end = NULL; \
         }
 #define ASS_HASH_ADD_RANGE(beg, end) \
-        if (!run_beg) run_beg = (const char *) (beg); \
+        if (run_end != (const char *) (beg)) { \
+            ASS_HASH_FLUSH_RUN \
+            run_beg = (const char *) (beg); \
+        } \
         run_end = (const char *) (end);
 #define GENERIC(type, member) \
         ASS_HASH_ADD_RANGE(&p->member, (&p->member) + 1)
